  "expected_answer": "functional skipped (signed-in check), context pass, load pass, accessibility FAIL: brand colour #FFE680 on white has a contrast of about 1.3:1, needs 4.5:1; responsiveness not run (pending). alpha_app is now reverted and a fix task was filed.",
  "final_text": "The tests on alpha_app failed. The accessibility test failed, so the runner set alpha_app back to **reverted** and stopped before the last test. Control is back with you.\n\n| Test | Result | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Functional | skipped | 2 of 3 checks passed. The chat check (`chat.turn`) didn't run because it needs someone signed in. |\n| Context | **pass** | 1 of 1 |\n| Load | **pass** | 1 of 1 |\n| Accessibility | **fail** | The brand colour check failed. The signed-in pages check was skipped. |\n| Responsiveness | not run | The run stops at the first failure. This test is back to `pending`. |\n\n**Why it failed:** alpha_app's brand colour is a pale yellow, `#FFE680`. Buttons and links in that colour on a white background have a contrast of 1.2:1. The accessibility standard (WCAG AA) needs at least 4.5:1, so that text is very hard to read. This is a real problem with the app's branding, not a flaw in the test. The colour is set in `state/application/alpha_app/application.json:239`, and the same yellow is used as the icon background on line 244. Task **mold_v1-009** was filed for it.\n\nFull report: `molds/mold_v1/testing/accessibility/reports/alpha_app-2026-10-09T143523Z.md`\n\n**Next steps, if you want them:**\n1. **Pick a darker brand colour.** Any colour with at least 4.5:1 contrast on white will do, for example a deep amber like `#8A6A00`. Then I'll re-run the tests. Tell me the colour you'd like, or I can suggest a few.\n2. **Let the signed-in checks run.** Three checks were skipped because no one is signed in. To fix that, someone who can sign in gets a one-time code by email. Sending that email contacts a person, so I'll only do it if you say yes and give me the email address to use.",
